EMK wrote:
> After my first excursion into the game and finding that I was dead of 
> starvation within 10 minutes (real time), I figure that I need to get a 
> better handle on how time works in this game. 

Don't rely on food rations alone, eat monster corpses as well. But careful;
some monsters are poisonous or rotten, and you'll learn that as you proceed
in the game. If you're lacking food (including monster corpses) you will
find a lot in the mines which start somewhere between level 2 and 4 as a
second staircase down, and you'll recognise by the amorphous shape of the
map. As a last resort (if weak or worse) #pray. Wearing rings (or amulets)
will increase your demands for nutrition, casting spells as well as a few
other actions (like jumping).

> For instance, I get that 
> moving one space is considered a "turn," but how long is that exactly?  And 
> what other actions/commands take a "turn" to complete?  Which don't take any 
> time at all?  Are there any that take some fraction of a turn?

Waiting, not hitting any key, (real time) does not consume any time in game.
Inspection commands (like : / i ; $ = ) don't require time (in most cases)
as well as some more commands that are not performed to end or aborted.
Other actions require time; to take off or put on armor, eating a meal,
walking through the dungeon, and especially searching (if coupled with a
number the required time increases proportionally). (You've already gotten
responses and links elsethread that cover speed and turns.)

Most starvation deaths occurred to me when I've searched too much. Try to
get some impression about the dungeon layout principles to avoid unnecessary
searches.

